      "grammar": {
        "title": "Subject pronouns and the verb ser",
        "body": "Spanish usually drops the subject pronoun, because the verb ending already shows who is acting. \"Soy estudiante\" means \"I am a student.\" Add the pronoun only for emphasis or contrast.\n\nSer means \"to be\" for identity, origin and profession. It is irregular, so learn the forms as a set:\n\nyo soy\ntú eres\nél / ella / usted es\nnosotros somos\nustedes son\nellos / ellas son\n\nUsted is the formal \"you\" and takes the same form as él and ella. Ustedes is the plural \"you\" throughout Latin America; vosotros is mostly a Spain form and is skipped here.\n\nDo not use an article before a profession: \"Soy médico,\" not \"Soy un médico.\" To negate, put no before the verb: \"No soy profesor.\"\n\nNouns for people often change -o to -a for a woman: hermano, hermana; amigo, amiga; médico, médica. Profesor adds -a: profesora. Estudiante stays the same for both.",
        "examples": [
          {"es": "Yo soy estudiante.", "en": "I am a student."},
          {"es": "¿Tú eres médico?", "en": "Are you a doctor?"},
          {"es": "Ella es mi madre.", "en": "She is my mother."},
          {"es": "Nosotros somos amigos.", "en": "We are friends."},
          {"es": "Ellos no son hermanos.", "en": "They are not brothers."},
          {"es": "Hola, me llamo Marta. Mucho gusto.", "en": "Hello, my name is Marta. Nice to meet you."},
          {"es": "¿De dónde eres? Soy de México.", "en": "Where are you from? I am from Mexico."}
        ]
      },
